.TH ATOI 2
.SH NAME
atoi, itoa \- number conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "atoi :: proc(s: string) -> (v: i64, ok: bool)"
.B "itoa :: proc(buf: []u8, v: i64) -> string"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Atoi
parses
.I s
as a decimal integer, with an optional leading
.B +
or
.BR \- ,
and answers the value and
.BR true .
Anything else in
.IR s ,
including a fraction, an exponent or trailing text, answers
.B false
and a value of zero.
There is no leading white space to skip: a caller that split its
argument on spaces already removed it.
.PP
.I Itoa
writes
.I v
in decimal into
.I buf
and answers the digits written, as a string aliasing
.IR buf .
.I Buf
must hold the sign and every digit; twenty-four bytes is enough for any
.BR i64 .
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR Tail (1)
parses its
.B \-n
argument:
.IP
.EX
if v, ok := libuser.atoi(args[1]); ok {
	last = v
}
.EE
.PP
.IR Uniq (1)
prints a repeat count:
.IP
.EX
num: [24]u8
s := libuser.itoa(num[:], repeats)
libuser.bio_puts(out, s)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/lines.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bio (2),
.IR tail (1),
.IR uniq (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Atoi
answers
.B false
for an empty string, a bare sign, or any character that is not a decimal
digit.
.SH BUGS
There is no floating-point conversion, and no base other than ten.
An
.I atoi
that overflows
.B i64
wraps rather than reports it.
